Reference quirk. The tree is the reference's own as of ticket 36: nothing
nests inside `\xo`, so `\em` is the `\xo`'s sibling, and the `\+pn` inside the
`\em` is a child because the author wrote `\+`. What is left is the `status`
attribute: Paratext marks the `\+pn` `status="invalid"` — a cross-reference
note holds cross-reference content, and `\pn` is not that — where the parser
reports through diagnostics and writes no such attribute. Every other
reference file that uses `status` belongs to a case that is decided on its
error diagnostics before the USX is compared at all; this one reports nothing,
because the input is nesting the author asked for.

--- origin.xml
+++ origin.xml
@@ -5,7 +5,7 @@
     <note caller="+" style="x">
       <char style="xo">1.1 </char>
       <char style="em">
-        <char style="pn" status="invalid">name</char> stuff </char>
+        <char style="pn">name</char> stuff </char>
     </note> <verse number="1" style="v" sid="GEN 1:1" />something<verse eid="GEN 1:1" /></para>
   <chapter eid="GEN 1" />
 </usx>
\ No newline at end of file
